// EXPECT: [E064]
// ★ A SLICE IS NOT A NICHE SOURCE, and the reason is representation, not reasoning (D-63).
//
// The argument for it is sound: a slice is (ptr, len) and its ptr is never NULL (D-N2), so
// the zero page really is spare. What does not follow is that a marker can be STORED there —
// a slice is a two-word struct and a sentinel is an integer, so there is no `typedef
// <backing> U;` to write and no `(U)(uintptr_t)0` for C to perform.
//
// The compiler used to believe the argument. It ACCEPTED this program and emitted
// `typedef Slice_u8_0 __U_t2_none;`, naming a type nothing defines — C that gcc rejects, from
// a compiler whose whole claim is prove-or-reject. Nothing caught it because no corpus
// program had this shape; it surfaced only when a SECOND backend was written and the two were
// compared on it.
//
// Refusing is the correct answer while the representation does not exist: E064 is
// prove-or-reject for layout, and "I cannot make this zero-cost" is exactly what it says.
extern proc libc_printf(fmt *u8, ...) i32
func pick(b bool) u8[:0] | none {
    if b { return "hi" }
    return none
}
func main() i32 {
    var r u8[:0] | none = pick(true)
    case r {
        none: libc_printf("none\n")
        else: libc_printf("some\n")
    }
    return 0
}
